- HANDLE LIVE RACE ERRORS, COMPLETION, WINNERS, AND BET RESULTS WITH RXJS AND RESOURCES.

- Angular Prerequisite "Advanced observables" chapter

- Ponies are now moving, with real data coming from a server in real time, and that’s great!
    As we strive to make an application of professional quality grade, we now have to handle errors,
    completion, unsubscription, etc…​Right now we subscribe to the Observable returned by the live
    method, ⚠️but we don’t act when we have no more events coming in.

    We should do a few things when a pony (or several ones) wins the race:
    👉 complete the observable to inform the subscribers that the race is over
    👉 stop the "running animation" of our ponies
    👉 display a message with the winners of the race, and if the bet is won or lost.

    👉Similarly, we should handle potential errors (the connection could break for example), with at
    least an error message if something goes wrong.

    This gives us an opportunity to learn a few advanced tricks with RxJS. Let’s go 💪!


1️⃣ Now we are going to slightly refactor our Live. ⚠️Right now, we always subscribe to the live
    positions, even if the race is already finished. We need to do better, and ✅only subscribe to
    these positions when the race is not finished yet.
    
    To do so, change the params function of the liveRace resource to:
    👉 return undefined if the initial race is not loaded yet;
    👉 return undefined if the race is finished;
    👉 return the race otherwise.

        params: () => {
            if (!this.initialRace.hasValue()) {
                return undefined;
            }
            const race = this.initialRace.value();
            return race.status === 'FINISHED' ? undefined : race; 
        }



2️⃣ We also need to handle the potential errors during the live✅. Add a liveError?: boolean property to
    our RaceModelWithPositions interface, and add a catchError operator after the startWith operator
    in the stream of liveRace to return of({ …​race, poniesWithPosition: [], liveError: true }) in case
    of an error.

        interface RaceModelWithPositions extends RaceModel {
            poniesWithPosition: Array<PonyWithPositionModel>;
            liveError?: boolean; 👈
        }
        // omitted
        export class Live {
           // omitted
            private readonly liveRace = rxResource<RaceModelWithPositions, RaceModel | undefined>({
                params: () => {
                    // omitted
                },
                stream: ({ params: race }) =>
                this.raceService.live(race.id).pipe(
                    map(live => ({ ...race, poniesWithPosition: live.ponies, status: live.status })),
                    startWith({ ...race, poniesWithPosition: [] }),
                    catchError(() => of({ ...​race, poniesWithPosition: [], liveError: true })) 👈
                )
            });
            // omitted
        }



3️⃣ Now, we are going to add two computed fields in our Live:
   1. winners, an array of PonyWithPositionModel, with all the ponies that have reached the finish
    line.
   2. betWon, a boolean that will be true if the user has bet on one of the pony contained in the
    winners computed field.
    
    🧠To find the race winners, use the poniesWithPosition array and filter the ponies with a position
    greater than or equal to 100 (use the filter operator on the poniesWithPosition array). 
    🧠To find the bet result, check if the winners array contains a pony with the same id as the
    betPonyId property of the raceModel. You can do that using the some operator available on arrays
    (array.some(predicate) returns true if at least one element satisfies the predicate function).

        protected readonly winners = computed<PonyWithPositionModel[] | undefined>(() =>
            this.raceModel()?.poniesWithPosition.filter(p => p.position >= 100)
        );
        protected readonly betWon = computed<boolean | undefined>(() =>
            this.winners()?.some(p => p.id === this.raceModel()?.betPonyId)
        );



